    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "When you're trying to focus on something—like watching your show or reading—do you find it hard to keep your mind on it, or is it more that your body just needs to move even though your mind might be willing to sit still?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S002",
      "patient_response": "Well, it's... it's definitely my mind too, not just my tired old bones. I mean, I can sit down with the senior center newsletter—they mail it out every month, usually on a Tuesday, though last month it didn't come until Thursday because of the holiday. And don't get me started on the post office these days, I swear they lose half my mail. My niece sent me a birthday card with a check in it and it took three weeks to get here. Three weeks! \n\nBut anyway, I'll sit down with the newsletter, or maybe the choir schedule for church, and I start out fine. I'm looking right at it. But then after just a few minutes, I realize my eyes have just been going over the same lines again and again, and I have absolutely no idea what I just read. It's like my brain just packs up and leaves the room. Just like that new choir director we have, he's always wandering off in the middle of practice to take a phone call. I don't know who he's talking to, but it's certainly not helping us learn the Easter cantata, I'll tell you that much.",
