    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Have you always had this trouble focusing and sitting still, even when you were younger—say, in school or as a young adult? Or is this something that's more recently started bothering you?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, well... no, it's not new at all. Lord, I think I came out of the womb this way! Even back in grade school, I remember my teachers—Mrs. Higgins in third grade, she was a strict one, wore these awful horn-rimmed glasses and always smelled like mothballs and peppermint. She used to write on my report cards that I was a bright girl but just so careless. I was always losing my spelling papers or talking out of turn. \n\nI sat next to a boy named Tommy Jenkins, and Tommy was always bringing frogs to school in his pockets. Can you imagine? Just right there in his corduroys. One time, one got loose during a math test, and I was the one who got in trouble because I couldn't stop talking about it! Mrs. Higgins was always having to remind me to stay in my seat or finish my worksheets. I just couldn't sit still, not even then. \n\nAnd Tommy, well, he ended up moving away in the fifth grade, his father got a job at the Ford plant up in Detroit. I always wondered what happened to him. Probably still collecting frogs, if I had to guess. But yes, it's always been this way for me, losing things and needing to be told to just sit down and finish what I started.",
